\b How websites use cookies\b0\par
Cookies are small pieces of information that a website saves in a visitor's web browser. When someone visits a website, the site can store details such as a session identifier, language choice, items in a shopping cart, or preferences. The browser sends matching cookies back to that website during later requests, allowing the site to recognize the browser and remember information between pages or visits. For example, a shopping website may use a cookie to keep items in a cart, while another site may remember a user's preferred language. Cookies can make a website easier to use, but they can also help websites track browsing activity over time.\par
\par
There are positive and negative aspects to using cookies. On the positive side, cookies can keep users signed in during a session, remember useful settings, preserve shopping carts, and make websites more convenient. They can also help website owners understand how visitors use their pages and improve the experience. On the negative side, cookies may collect information about browsing behavior, create privacy concerns, and be used by advertising or analytics companies to follow people across different websites. If a device is shared or not secured, stored information may also be exposed to another person. In my opinion, cookies are ethical when websites use them transparently, collect only the information they actually need, protect that information, and give people meaningful choices about non-essential tracking. I do not think it is ethical to secretly track people or collect more information than is necessary without their knowledge or consent. Users should be able to understand what is being saved and control optional cookies.\par
